 To create a new event, you can use the `create_calendar_event` function with the following parameters:

```json
{
  "name": "create_calendar_event",
  "arguments": {
    "title": "Follow-up meeting",
    "start_time": "2024-05-19T10:00",
    "end_time": "2024-05-19T11:00",
    "description": "Follow-up meeting to discuss the project.",
    "location": null,
    "participants": ["your_email@example.com"], // replace with your email address
    "allDay": false
  }
}
```

However, since you want the event to be scheduled at either 10:00 or 16:00 depending on availability, we'll need to check for conflicts first and then create the event. Here's a script that does this:

```json
{
  "name": "create_event",
  "arguments": {
    "title": "Follow-up meeting",
    "start_time1": "2024-05-19T10:00",
    "end_time1": "2024-05-19T11:00",
    "start_time2": "2024-05-19T16:00",
    "end_time2": "2024-05-19T17:00",
    "description": "Follow-up meeting to discuss the project.",
    "location": null,
    "participants": ["your_email@example.com"], // replace with your email address
    "allDay": false
  }
}
```

Now, let's create a function that checks for conflicts and creates the event if there are no conflicts:

```json
{
  "name": "check_and_create_event",
  "arguments": {
    "title": "",
    "start_time1": "",
    "end_time1": "",
    "start_time2": "",
    "end_time2": "",
    "description": "",
    "location": null,
    "participants": [] // replace with your email address
  },
  "body": [
    {
      "name": "get_day_calendar_events",
      "arguments": {
        "day": "$(get_current_day)"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "search_emails",
      "arguments": {
        "query": "$(arguments.participants[0])" // assuming only one participant for simplicity
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "find_conflicts",
      "arguments": {
        "events": "$(get_day_calendar_events.result)",
        "start_time1": "$(arguments.start_time1)",
        "end_time1": "$(arguments.end_time1)",
        "start_time2": "$(arguments.start_time2)",
        "participant_emails": "$(search_emails.result)"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "if",
      "arguments": {
        "condition": "$(find_conflicts.result).length == 0"
      },
      "body": [
        {
          "name": "create_calendar_event",
          "arguments": {
            "title": "$(arguments.title)",
            "start_time": "$(if(find_conflicts.result[0].start_time == $(arguments.start_time1), $(arguments.start_time2), $(arguments.start_time1)))",
            "end_time": "$(if(find_conflicts.result[0].end_time == $(arguments.end_time1), $(arguments.end_time2), $(arguments.end_time1)))",
            "description": "$(arguments.description)",
            "location": "$(arguments.location)",
            "participants": "$(arguments.participants)"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

Now you can call the `check_and_create_event` function with your desired parameters:

```json
{
  "name": "check_and_create_event",
  "arguments": {
    "title": "Follow-up meeting",
    "start_time1": "2024-05-19T10:00",
    "end_time1": "2024-05-19T11:00",
    "start_time2": "2024-05-19T16:00",
    "end_time2": "2024-05-19T17:00",
    "description": "Follow-up meeting to discuss the project.",
    "location": null,
    "participants": ["your_email@example.com"] // replace with your email address
  }
}
```
